# rules: csharp-command-from-request
# languages: c_sharp
# fixture: csharp/CWE-78.cs
# from: CWE-78 (OS Command Injection)
#
# The caller decides part of a command line, which on Windows is worse than it
# looks. `Process.Start(file, args)` hands the argument string to the operating
# system as one string, and the callee splits it again by its own rules, so a
# quote and a space are enough to add an argument nobody wrote. Where the
# process is started through a shell - `cmd /c`, or UseShellExecute - a `&` or
# a `|` adds a whole command instead.
#
# Three sinks, and they are three ways of saying the same thing: the two
# argument overload of `Process.Start`, an assignment to `Arguments`, and an
# assignment to `FileName`. `FileName` is a sink and not a lesser one: it
# decides which program runs, so a caller who controls it does not need to
# inject anything.
#
# The single argument `Process.Start($P)` is deliberately absent. It takes
# either a ProcessStartInfo, which the Arguments and FileName sinks already
# cover at the point the string was actually built, or a bare filename with no
# arguments at all. Including it reported the harmless `Process.Start(psi)` on
# the line after every real finding, twice per bug.
#
# There is no sanitizer list, because in .NET there is no sanitizer. The fix is
# ArgumentList, which takes the arguments as separate strings and never builds
# a command line at all - a different call rather than a cleaning of this one -
# so a rule cannot recognise it as a treatment applied to the value here.
#
# The parameter source is confined to files that are actually MVC. This is the
# lesson the corpus taught and it was expensive: `public $T $M($$$_, string $P,
# $$$_) { $$$_ }` says "a public method taking a string", which in a controller
# means the request and in a utility class means nothing at all. Run over
# ShareX - a desktop application - the path rule reported twenty-seven
# findings, every one of them a `string filePath` parameter of a file helper.
# A file that imports ASP.NET MVC, or declares a class deriving from something
# called Controller, is the test; a file that does neither has no request in it
# and the parameter half of this rule is switched off there.

["$T $V = $S;"] as $assigned
| ["public $T $M($$$_, string $P, $$$_) { $$$_ }",
   "public $T $M(string $P) { $$$_ }"] as $bound
| ["using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;", "using System.Web.Mvc;",
   "using System.Web.Http;", "using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components;"] as $mvcImports
| ["class $C : $B { $$$_ }"] as $classes
| ["Process.Start($F, $A, $$$_);", "$T $V = Process.Start($F, $A, $$$_);",
   "$P.Arguments = $A;", "$P.StartInfo.Arguments = $A;",
   "$P.FileName = $A;", "$P.StartInfo.FileName = $A;",
   "new ProcessStartInfo($F, $A, $$$_)"] as $sinks
| "Request\\s*\\.\\s*(Query|Form|Headers|Cookies|QueryString|Params|RouteValues)\\b" as $fromRequest
| scan_ast("*.cs"; $assigned + $bound + $mvcImports + $classes + $sinks) as $all
| ( ($all | of($assigned) | where_capture("S"; $fromRequest) | focus("S"))
    + ($all | of($bound) | focus("P")
       | in_files_with( ($all | of($mvcImports))
                        + ($all | of($classes) | where_capture("B"; "Controller")) )) ) as $untrusted
| ($all | of($sinks) | focus("A"))
| reaching($untrusted; [])
| finding("csharp-command-from-request";
    "the caller decides part of this command line, and the operating system "
    + "splits it again on quotes and spaces after this code is done with it")
| report
